1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
Last night I told you about the

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,517
week I was born.

3
00:00:03,697 --> 00:00:06,321
The telegram that said my father was

4
00:00:06,321 --> 00:00:07,633
missing in France.

5
00:00:07,813 --> 00:00:08,997
And Mrs.

6
00:00:09,177 --> 00:00:10,401
Pruitt next door,

7
00:00:10,401 --> 00:00:12,057
holding me on her knee,

8
00:00:12,057 --> 00:00:14,576
counting the months on her fingers.

9
00:00:14,756 --> 00:00:17,914
October, November, December.

10
00:00:18,094 --> 00:00:20,991
Jack, you asked me what she was

11
00:00:20,991 --> 00:00:21,832
counting.

12
00:00:22,012 --> 00:00:22,860
Well.

13
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:25,536
Tonight you'll start to understand.

14
00:00:25,885 --> 00:00:28,509
Come closer, both of you.

15
00:00:28,689 --> 00:00:31,501
Emma, give your brother some of that

16
00:00:31,501 --> 00:00:32,126
blanket.

17
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:34,587
This part of the story happened in

18
00:00:34,587 --> 00:00:36,733
the fall of nineteen forty-four,

19
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:38,209
when I was a baby in a

20
00:00:38,209 --> 00:00:39,551
crib under a window.

21
00:00:39,731 --> 00:00:41,797
So I don't remember any of it.

22
00:00:41,977 --> 00:00:44,178
Grandma Pearl told me,

23
00:00:44,178 --> 00:00:45,379
years later.

24
00:00:45,559 --> 00:00:47,701
And some of it I found out

25
00:00:47,701 --> 00:00:50,173
for myself, in a way I'll tell

26
00:00:50,173 --> 00:00:51,409
you about much,

27
00:00:51,409 --> 00:00:52,316
much later.

28
00:00:52,665 --> 00:00:55,298
In a town like Mansfield in those

29
00:00:55,298 --> 00:00:59,128
days, sweethearts, news traveled faster than the

30
00:00:59,128 --> 00:00:59,527
mail.

31
00:00:59,707 --> 00:01:01,323
There was no internet,

32
00:01:01,323 --> 00:01:03,085
no phone in your pocket.

33
00:01:03,265 --> 00:01:04,914
There was something better.

34
00:01:05,094 --> 00:01:06,238
The party line,

35
00:01:06,238 --> 00:01:07,762
the church basement,

36
00:01:07,762 --> 00:01:10,506
and the beauty parlor on Park Avenue

37
00:01:10,506 --> 00:01:10,887
West.

38
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:12,973
And by the end of August,

39
00:01:12,973 --> 00:01:15,718
all three of them were talking about

40
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:17,395
Dorothy Miller's baby.

41
00:01:17,744 --> 00:01:18,766
Mrs.

42
00:01:18,946 --> 00:01:21,384
Pruitt never said anything out loud.

43
00:01:21,564 --> 00:01:23,341
She was too clever for that.

44
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:25,726
She just asked questions.

45
00:01:25,906 --> 00:01:28,188
Isn't it a blessing the baby came

46
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:28,948
so healthy?

47
00:01:29,128 --> 00:01:31,624
Didn't Walter ship out in October?

48
00:01:31,804 --> 00:01:34,760
My, Frank certainly is a help to

49
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:36,054
her, isn't he?

50
00:01:36,234 --> 00:01:38,649
And then she'd smile and sip her

51
00:01:38,649 --> 00:01:41,290
coffee, and let the other ladies do

52
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:42,422
the arithmetic.

53
00:01:42,771 --> 00:01:44,202
My mother heard it,

54
00:01:44,202 --> 00:01:44,954
of course.

55
00:01:45,134 --> 00:01:46,423
You always hear it.

56
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:47,629
At the A and P,

57
00:01:47,629 --> 00:01:50,296
two women stopped talking when she came

58
00:01:50,296 --> 00:01:52,280
down the aisle with me in the

59
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:52,895
carriage.

60
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:56,626
At church, Reverend Albrecht shook her hand

61
00:01:56,626 --> 00:01:58,855
a little too long and said,

62
00:01:58,855 --> 00:02:01,331
we are all praying for Walter,

63
00:02:01,331 --> 00:02:01,992
Dorothy.

64
00:02:02,172 --> 00:02:03,379
All of us.

65
00:02:03,559 --> 00:02:05,280
And she said thank you,

66
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:07,449
and held me a little tighter.

67
00:02:07,798 --> 00:02:09,936
And all that time there was no

68
00:02:09,936 --> 00:02:11,432
news about my father.

69
00:02:11,612 --> 00:02:12,936
Not one word.

70
00:02:13,116 --> 00:02:15,614
Every morning Mother walked to the end

71
00:02:15,614 --> 00:02:17,981
of the porch and watched the mailman

72
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:19,362
come up Maple Street.

73
00:02:19,542 --> 00:02:21,539
Every morning he shook his head.

74
00:02:21,719 --> 00:02:23,166
Missing in action,

75
00:02:23,166 --> 00:02:24,935
the telegram had said.

76
00:02:25,115 --> 00:02:26,748
In nineteen forty-four,

77
00:02:26,748 --> 00:02:29,872
sweethearts, there were tens of thousands of

78
00:02:29,872 --> 00:02:33,068
American families waiting on those two words.

79
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:35,966
You see, the fighting in France that

80
00:02:35,966 --> 00:02:37,736
summer had been terrible.

81
00:02:37,916 --> 00:02:41,053
After D-Day, our boys pushed through the

82
00:02:41,053 --> 00:02:42,777
hedgerows of Normandy,

83
00:02:42,777 --> 00:02:43,954
field by field.

84
00:02:44,134 --> 00:02:46,294
In the last week of July they

85
00:02:46,294 --> 00:02:47,934
finally broke through,

86
00:02:47,934 --> 00:02:50,020
near a town called Saint-Lô.

87
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,232
Thousands of men got separated from their

88
00:02:53,232 --> 00:02:55,006
units in that confusion.

89
00:02:55,186 --> 00:02:57,206
Some turned up a week later.

90
00:02:57,386 --> 00:02:59,786
Some turned up in a German prison

91
00:02:59,786 --> 00:03:00,150
camp.

92
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:02,431
And some never turned up at all.

93
00:03:02,781 --> 00:03:05,129
Mother kept a map of France on

94
00:03:05,129 --> 00:03:06,459
the kitchen wall,

95
00:03:06,459 --> 00:03:08,494
torn out of Life magazine.

96
00:03:08,674 --> 00:03:11,427
Every night, after the radio news,

97
00:03:11,427 --> 00:03:14,019
she put a pin wherever they said

98
00:03:14,019 --> 00:03:15,558
the Americans were.

99
00:03:15,738 --> 00:03:16,644
Paris.

100
00:03:16,824 --> 00:03:18,774
Then the border of Belgium.

101
00:03:18,954 --> 00:03:21,005
Then a forest called the Hürtgen,

102
00:03:21,005 --> 00:03:23,304
where the fighting went on for months

103
00:03:23,304 --> 00:03:24,608
in the freezing rain.

104
00:03:24,788 --> 00:03:27,192
She'd stand there with the pins in

105
00:03:27,192 --> 00:03:30,021
her mouth, the way she held clothespins,

106
00:03:30,021 --> 00:03:31,859
and just look at that map.

107
00:03:32,208 --> 00:03:33,613
Frank kept coming.

108
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:36,514
Every evening after his shift at the

109
00:03:36,514 --> 00:03:37,346
steel mill.

110
00:03:37,526 --> 00:03:39,760
He fixed the porch step that had

111
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:42,274
been loose since nineteen forty-one.

112
00:03:42,454 --> 00:03:45,135
He brought coal for the furnace when

113
00:03:45,135 --> 00:03:46,773
the coal man was late.

114
00:03:46,953 --> 00:03:49,315
He brought a pound of real butter

115
00:03:49,315 --> 00:03:50,317
from a friend,

116
00:03:50,317 --> 00:03:53,323
and Grandma Pearl didn't ask which friend,

117
00:03:53,323 --> 00:03:55,899
because in those days you didn't ask

118
00:03:55,899 --> 00:03:57,832
where the butter came from.

119
00:03:58,181 --> 00:03:59,493
And he held me.

120
00:03:59,673 --> 00:04:02,332
That's what Pearl remembered most.

121
00:04:02,512 --> 00:04:04,595
Frank would pick me up out of

122
00:04:04,595 --> 00:04:06,534
the crib and walk me up and

123
00:04:06,534 --> 00:04:07,970
down the front room,

124
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:10,627
singing, Mairzy Doats and Dozy Doats.

125
00:04:10,807 --> 00:04:13,270
That silly song that was on every

126
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:14,465
radio that year.

127
00:04:14,645 --> 00:04:16,839
And I would stop crying.

128
00:04:17,019 --> 00:04:19,805
For him I always stopped crying.

129
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:22,475
Pearl said that was the first thing

130
00:04:22,475 --> 00:04:23,898
that frightened her.

131
00:04:24,247 --> 00:04:26,343
One night Pearl said to him,

132
00:04:26,343 --> 00:04:28,514
very quietly, in the kitchen,

133
00:04:28,514 --> 00:04:30,610
Francis, people are talking.

134
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:33,698
And Frank laughed his big laugh and

135
00:04:33,698 --> 00:04:36,107
said, Ma, people always talk.

136
00:04:36,287 --> 00:04:39,122
Somebody has to look after my brother's

137
00:04:39,122 --> 00:04:39,631
family.

138
00:04:39,811 --> 00:04:41,016
And Pearl said,

139
00:04:41,016 --> 00:04:42,783
your brother's family.

140
00:04:42,963 --> 00:04:43,822
Yes.

141
00:04:44,002 --> 00:04:45,140
Remember that.

142
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:47,410
And Frank stopped laughing.

143
00:04:47,759 --> 00:04:50,380
Now, every kitchen in America had a

144
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:52,926
set of canisters on the counter in

145
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:53,750
those days.

146
00:04:53,930 --> 00:04:56,152
Flour, sugar, coffee,

147
00:04:56,152 --> 00:04:58,586
tea, from big to small,

148
00:04:58,586 --> 00:04:59,538
in a row.

149
00:04:59,718 --> 00:05:02,818
Ours were white enamel with red lids.

150
00:05:02,998 --> 00:05:05,974
And since sugar and coffee were rationed,

151
00:05:05,974 --> 00:05:08,369
the flour tin was the biggest and

152
00:05:08,369 --> 00:05:10,909
the fullest, and the one nobody but

153
00:05:10,909 --> 00:05:12,506
my mother ever opened.

154
00:05:12,856 --> 00:05:15,222
In the second week of November,

155
00:05:15,222 --> 00:05:17,512
Grandma Pearl decided to bake.

156
00:05:17,692 --> 00:05:19,325
It was her birthday,

157
00:05:19,325 --> 00:05:21,692
and she wanted a sponge cake,

158
00:05:21,692 --> 00:05:22,835
war or no war.

159
00:05:23,015 --> 00:05:25,398
She saved up her sugar stamps for

160
00:05:25,398 --> 00:05:25,976
a month.

161
00:05:26,156 --> 00:05:27,825
And on Saturday morning,

162
00:05:27,825 --> 00:05:30,190
while my mother was at the market,

163
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:32,486
Pearl took down the flour tin and

164
00:05:32,486 --> 00:05:34,364
dug in the big metal scoop.

165
00:05:34,544 --> 00:05:37,311
And the scoop hit something that wasn't

166
00:05:37,311 --> 00:05:37,737
flour.

167
00:05:38,086 --> 00:05:39,675
It was an envelope,

168
00:05:39,675 --> 00:05:40,929
folded in half,

169
00:05:40,929 --> 00:05:43,438
pushed all the way down to the

170
00:05:43,438 --> 00:05:45,946
bottom and covered with flour.

171
00:05:46,126 --> 00:05:48,481
Pearl pulled it out and blew it

172
00:05:48,481 --> 00:05:48,785
off.

173
00:05:48,965 --> 00:05:50,556
There was no stamp on it.

174
00:05:50,736 --> 00:05:52,257
No address.

175
00:05:52,437 --> 00:05:54,724
It had never gone through the mail.

176
00:05:54,904 --> 00:05:57,564
Somebody had put it into my mother's

177
00:05:57,564 --> 00:05:57,934
hand.

178
00:05:58,114 --> 00:05:59,761
Or into her mailbox,

179
00:05:59,761 --> 00:06:00,749
in the dark.

180
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:03,326
Pearl told me she stood in that

181
00:06:03,326 --> 00:06:05,696
kitchen for a long time with that

182
00:06:05,696 --> 00:06:07,276
envelope in her hands.

183
00:06:07,456 --> 00:06:09,278
She said she wanted to open it

184
00:06:09,278 --> 00:06:11,647
more than she had ever wanted anything.

185
00:06:11,827 --> 00:06:13,453
And she said she didn't,

186
00:06:13,453 --> 00:06:15,892
because she was a churchgoing woman,

187
00:06:15,892 --> 00:06:17,586
and a letter is a letter.

188
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:20,101
So she put it back exactly where

189
00:06:20,101 --> 00:06:22,509
it was, and poured the flour back

190
00:06:22,509 --> 00:06:24,699
over it, and she made her cake

191
00:06:24,699 --> 00:06:26,961
with flour from the neighbor's.

192
00:06:27,311 --> 00:06:30,039
But she looked at the handwriting first.

193
00:06:30,219 --> 00:06:31,438
Just the front.

194
00:06:31,618 --> 00:06:34,198
One word, my mother's name,

195
00:06:34,198 --> 00:06:36,014
Dorothy, in pencil,

196
00:06:36,014 --> 00:06:38,212
in a big slanting hand.

197
00:06:38,392 --> 00:06:40,842
And Pearl knew that handwriting.

198
00:06:41,022 --> 00:06:43,497
She had signed every report card that

199
00:06:43,497 --> 00:06:45,504
handwriting ever brought home.

200
00:06:45,854 --> 00:06:48,860
That week was the presidential election.

201
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:51,815
November seventh, nineteen forty-four.

202
00:06:51,995 --> 00:06:55,235
President Roosevelt was running for a fourth

203
00:06:55,235 --> 00:06:58,253
term, against Governor Dewey of New York.

204
00:06:58,433 --> 00:07:01,719
Nobody had ever been president four times.

205
00:07:01,899 --> 00:07:04,062
Father was a Roosevelt man,

206
00:07:04,062 --> 00:07:07,186
and Mother voted for Roosevelt for him,

207
00:07:07,186 --> 00:07:10,311
she said, because he couldn't vote from

208
00:07:10,311 --> 00:07:11,593
wherever he was.

209
00:07:11,942 --> 00:07:13,898
Frank was a Dewey man,

210
00:07:13,898 --> 00:07:14,787
of course.

211
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:17,720
Frank was always for whoever my father

212
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:18,589
was against.

213
00:07:18,769 --> 00:07:21,562
They had argued about Roosevelt at every

214
00:07:21,562 --> 00:07:24,284
Thanksgiving since nineteen thirty-six.

215
00:07:24,464 --> 00:07:26,885
And on election night Frank sat in

216
00:07:26,885 --> 00:07:29,734
our front room listening to the returns,

217
00:07:29,734 --> 00:07:31,941
with his feet up on my father's

218
00:07:31,941 --> 00:07:34,576
footstool, smoking my father's brand.

219
00:07:34,756 --> 00:07:36,573
And when Roosevelt won,

220
00:07:36,573 --> 00:07:37,916
Frank said, well,

221
00:07:37,916 --> 00:07:40,364
Walt won't be happy about that.

222
00:07:40,544 --> 00:07:42,650
And then nobody said anything,

223
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:45,317
because nobody knew if Walt would ever

224
00:07:45,317 --> 00:07:46,581
be anything again.

225
00:07:46,931 --> 00:07:50,623
And then, eight days before Thanksgiving,

226
00:07:50,623 --> 00:07:51,343
it came.

227
00:07:51,523 --> 00:07:53,787
Not a telegram this time.

228
00:07:53,967 --> 00:07:55,912
A thin blue V-mail letter,

229
00:07:55,912 --> 00:07:58,605
with an Army post office address and

230
00:07:58,605 --> 00:08:01,223
a little red stamp from the censor.

231
00:08:01,403 --> 00:08:03,500
Mother saw it from the porch and

232
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:05,859
ran barefoot down the steps into the

233
00:08:05,859 --> 00:08:07,104
street in November.

234
00:08:07,284 --> 00:08:08,213
Mrs.

235
00:08:08,393 --> 00:08:09,925
Pruitt saw that too.

236
00:08:10,275 --> 00:08:12,383
It was from a Red Cross nurse

237
00:08:12,383 --> 00:08:14,200
at a hospital in England.

238
00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:17,085
Your husband, Private Walter J.

239
00:08:17,265 --> 00:08:19,970
Miller, is a patient in this hospital.

240
00:08:20,150 --> 00:08:22,527
He was found on the twenty-ninth of

241
00:08:22,527 --> 00:08:24,633
July with wounds to his leg and

242
00:08:24,633 --> 00:08:26,806
his head, and he was without his

243
00:08:26,806 --> 00:08:28,776
identification for some time.

244
00:08:28,956 --> 00:08:30,500
He is recovering.

245
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:32,491
He asks me to write to say

246
00:08:32,491 --> 00:08:34,512
he is thinking of you and the

247
00:08:34,512 --> 00:08:35,556
baby every day.

248
00:08:35,906 --> 00:08:38,205
Thinking of you and the baby.

249
00:08:38,385 --> 00:08:40,748
You see, Mother had written to him

250
00:08:40,748 --> 00:08:42,347
about me in the spring,

251
00:08:42,347 --> 00:08:43,319
when she knew.

252
00:08:43,499 --> 00:08:45,547
So he knew there would be a

253
00:08:45,547 --> 00:08:45,926
baby.

254
00:08:46,106 --> 00:08:48,103
He just didn't know when.

255
00:08:48,283 --> 00:08:50,206
He was lying in a hospital bed

256
00:08:50,206 --> 00:08:52,513
in England with a bandage around his

257
00:08:52,513 --> 00:08:54,628
head, and he didn't know what day

258
00:08:54,628 --> 00:08:56,038
his daughter was born.

259
00:08:56,218 --> 00:08:57,414
Not yet.

260
00:08:57,764 --> 00:09:00,211
Mother read that letter out loud on

261
00:09:00,211 --> 00:09:02,378
the porch, and then she read it

262
00:09:02,378 --> 00:09:04,544
again, and then she sat down on

263
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:06,921
the top step and laughed and cried

264
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:08,109
at the same time.

265
00:09:08,289 --> 00:09:10,437
Pearl came out and held her.

266
00:09:10,617 --> 00:09:13,021
And the ladies came out of every

267
00:09:13,021 --> 00:09:14,675
house on Maple Street,

268
00:09:14,675 --> 00:09:16,854
and somebody started singing,

269
00:09:16,854 --> 00:09:18,507
and for one afternoon,

270
00:09:18,507 --> 00:09:21,437
sweethearts, everybody forgot to count.

271
00:09:21,787 --> 00:09:25,226
Thanksgiving that year was the happiest Grandma

272
00:09:25,226 --> 00:09:28,812
Pearl could remember since before the Depression.

273
00:09:28,992 --> 00:09:30,786
Turkey was hard to get,

274
00:09:30,786 --> 00:09:32,347
so we had a chicken.

275
00:09:32,527 --> 00:09:35,241
There was no sugar for pumpkin pie,

276
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:37,800
so Mother made it with corn syrup

277
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:38,808
and molasses.

278
00:09:38,988 --> 00:09:41,182
Frank brought a bottle of wine.

279
00:09:41,362 --> 00:09:42,651
The Pruitts came.

280
00:09:42,831 --> 00:09:44,068
And at the table,

281
00:09:44,068 --> 00:09:45,305
Pearl said grace,

282
00:09:45,305 --> 00:09:47,925
and she thanked God for bringing her

283
00:09:47,925 --> 00:09:49,599
son back from the dead.

284
00:09:49,949 --> 00:09:52,532
Frank raised his glass and said,

285
00:09:52,532 --> 00:09:53,177
to Walt.

286
00:09:53,357 --> 00:09:55,412
Everybody said, to Walt.

287
00:09:55,592 --> 00:09:58,541
And Pearl watched Frank drink his wine.

288
00:09:58,721 --> 00:10:01,050
She said he drank the whole glass

289
00:10:01,050 --> 00:10:02,250
without stopping.

290
00:10:02,430 --> 00:10:05,047
And then he excused himself and went

291
00:10:05,047 --> 00:10:07,083
out on the back porch in the

292
00:10:07,083 --> 00:10:08,755
cold, without his coat,

293
00:10:08,755 --> 00:10:11,009
and stood there in the dark for

294
00:10:11,009 --> 00:10:11,881
a long time.

295
00:10:12,230 --> 00:10:15,032
That night, after everyone had gone,

296
00:10:15,032 --> 00:10:16,665
Pearl couldn't sleep.

297
00:10:16,845 --> 00:10:18,666
Old people never can,

298
00:10:18,666 --> 00:10:20,746
sweethearts, you'll see.

299
00:10:20,926 --> 00:10:23,416
Around two in the morning she came

300
00:10:23,416 --> 00:10:25,687
downstairs for a glass of milk.

301
00:10:25,867 --> 00:10:27,956
And the kitchen light was off,

302
00:10:27,956 --> 00:10:29,907
but the kitchen wasn't dark.

303
00:10:30,087 --> 00:10:32,431
There was an orange glow coming from

304
00:10:32,431 --> 00:10:33,082
the stove.

305
00:10:33,432 --> 00:10:35,708
Our stove was a big old coal

306
00:10:35,708 --> 00:10:36,846
and gas range,

307
00:10:36,846 --> 00:10:39,446
the kind with a little iron door

308
00:10:39,446 --> 00:10:41,478
on the side for the fire.

309
00:10:41,658 --> 00:10:43,995
And my mother was kneeling in front

310
00:10:43,995 --> 00:10:46,465
of that little door in her nightgown.

311
00:10:46,645 --> 00:10:48,752
She had the flour tin open on

312
00:10:48,752 --> 00:10:50,279
the floor beside her.

313
00:10:50,459 --> 00:10:52,972
And she was feeding a piece of

314
00:10:52,972 --> 00:10:54,647
paper into the fire,

315
00:10:54,647 --> 00:10:57,076
slowly, one corner at a time.

316
00:10:57,426 --> 00:10:59,528
Pearl didn't say a word.

317
00:10:59,708 --> 00:11:01,767
She stood in the doorway in the

318
00:11:01,767 --> 00:11:02,100
dark.

319
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:04,746
And just before the last piece of

320
00:11:04,746 --> 00:11:06,615
paper went into the fire,

321
00:11:06,615 --> 00:11:08,932
Mother held it up to the light,

322
00:11:08,932 --> 00:11:10,801
to read it one more time.

323
00:11:10,981 --> 00:11:13,441
And Pearl saw the bottom of the

324
00:11:13,441 --> 00:11:13,837
page.

325
00:11:14,017 --> 00:11:15,271
There was no name.

326
00:11:15,451 --> 00:11:18,342
No love, no yours truly.

327
00:11:18,522 --> 00:11:20,032
Just one letter,

328
00:11:20,032 --> 00:11:22,202
written big, in pencil.

329
00:11:22,552 --> 00:11:23,296
F.

330
00:11:23,645 --> 00:11:26,560
Now, Emma, don't you look at me

331
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:27,500
like that.

332
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:29,456
I know what you're thinking.

333
00:11:29,636 --> 00:11:32,651
I'm only telling you what Grandma Pearl

334
00:11:32,651 --> 00:11:34,429
saw, with her own eyes,

335
00:11:34,429 --> 00:11:36,208
in nineteen forty-four.

336
00:11:36,388 --> 00:11:37,739
What it means,

337
00:11:37,739 --> 00:11:38,222
well.

338
00:11:38,402 --> 00:11:40,858
That took me forty years to find

339
00:11:40,858 --> 00:11:41,165
out.

340
00:11:41,345 --> 00:11:43,435
And you'll have to be patient too.

341
00:11:43,784 --> 00:11:46,432
Tomorrow I'll tell you about the spring

342
00:11:46,432 --> 00:11:47,994
of nineteen forty-five,

343
00:11:47,994 --> 00:11:50,234
and the night the whole town rang

344
00:11:50,234 --> 00:11:50,913
its bells.

345
00:11:51,093 --> 00:11:53,595
And the soldier who came home early,

346
00:11:53,595 --> 00:11:56,236
with something to say about my father.

347
00:11:56,416 --> 00:11:58,622
Goodnight, sweethearts.

348
00:11:58,802 --> 00:12:01,760
Grandma Ruth will be right here tomorrow

349
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:02,204
night.
